1
00:00:01,252 --> 00:00:01,920
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:02,293 --> 00:00:05,805
Thanks for joining the
Conference 42 Feature Flags 2026.

3
00:00:06,303 --> 00:00:09,215
I'm Pavan Boppidi, working as
a staff engineer at Walmart.

4
00:00:09,883 --> 00:00:11,514
Before we begin, a quick note.

5
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:16,268
The views and opinions in this
presentation are my own and do not

6
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:18,438
represent my previous or current employer.

7
00:00:19,418 --> 00:00:21,054
Little bit background about me.

8
00:00:21,359 --> 00:00:25,071
I'm a technology professional
with around 12 years of experience

9
00:00:25,077 --> 00:00:27,121
as a developer and an architect.

10
00:00:28,251 --> 00:00:33,272
Today, I will talk about human-in-the-loop
and feature flag autonomy in logistics.

11
00:00:34,177 --> 00:00:40,245
More recently, I have been focused on
an agentic AI in logistics systems.

12
00:00:41,187 --> 00:00:46,385
And throughout this journey, a question
that keeps coming up: Can an AI

13
00:00:46,410 --> 00:00:48,686
agent make this logistics decision?

14
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,971
Honestly, that is really easy today.

15
00:00:52,730 --> 00:00:57,584
We can already build agents that
plan, call tools, and act accordingly.

16
00:00:58,352 --> 00:01:05,253
The hard question is how to progressively
turn on agentic decision without turning

17
00:01:05,253 --> 00:01:10,754
off operational control Throughout
this session, I'll show you how feature

18
00:01:10,758 --> 00:01:15,723
flags and confidence-gated decisions fit
together into a single control plane.

19
00:01:17,059 --> 00:01:21,722
The goal here is not a full automation,
the goal here is a controlled autonomy.

20
00:01:22,343 --> 00:01:27,658
System can act when evidence supports
action, escalates when uncertainty

21
00:01:27,664 --> 00:01:32,520
or stakes demand a person, and
roll back when conditions change

22
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,767
Think back the last time you rolled
out a feature to one percent of the

23
00:01:36,767 --> 00:01:41,970
traffic, watched the telemetry, and
rolled it back without redeploying.

24
00:01:43,011 --> 00:01:46,848
If you have done, you already
know the power of runtime control.

25
00:01:47,716 --> 00:01:50,216
It is the reason this conference exists.

26
00:01:51,121 --> 00:01:53,338
Now change what's behind the flag.

27
00:01:54,521 --> 00:01:56,368
It isn't button color anymore.

28
00:01:57,032 --> 00:02:01,686
It is the agent that can choose
tools, sequence actions, spend

29
00:02:01,686 --> 00:02:03,240
money, and reroute the frame.

30
00:02:04,322 --> 00:02:06,156
The blast radius just got bigger.

31
00:02:07,574 --> 00:02:11,857
And look at what actually
Boolean tells you, on or off.

32
00:02:12,493 --> 00:02:16,158
It doesn't say who may act on
which decision and with what

33
00:02:16,161 --> 00:02:18,288
confidence or under what conditions.

34
00:02:19,694 --> 00:02:21,210
We got two questions here for you.

35
00:02:21,983 --> 00:02:24,549
Should this capability be exposed at all?

36
00:02:25,362 --> 00:02:26,456
That's a flag question.

37
00:02:27,488 --> 00:02:32,641
Second, given a capability is
exposed, should the agent execute

38
00:02:32,659 --> 00:02:34,345
this specific decision right now?

39
00:02:35,031 --> 00:02:36,233
That's a governance question.

40
00:02:37,216 --> 00:02:41,048
The rest of the talk I'll be c-
I'll be talking connecting those two

41
00:02:42,048 --> 00:02:43,193
Why logistics?

42
00:02:43,774 --> 00:02:46,430
Because decisions here don't live alone.

43
00:02:47,789 --> 00:02:50,239
Let's assume we have a disruption.

44
00:02:51,051 --> 00:02:56,194
A carrier shutdowns the service, which
will change a routing, and routing

45
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:58,049
changes when inventory arrives.

46
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:02,416
Inventory changes replenishment,
and replenishment changes what

47
00:03:02,416 --> 00:03:03,694
you can promise to a customer.

48
00:03:04,789 --> 00:03:07,582
One event, four decision loops fire.

49
00:03:08,296 --> 00:03:12,328
So the system is not solving a
one prediction, it is coordinating

50
00:03:12,335 --> 00:03:15,950
whole network, and disruptions
move through the network faster

51
00:03:15,974 --> 00:03:18,464
than people can plan by hand.

52
00:03:19,687 --> 00:03:24,544
A twenty-twenty-six annual review survey
similarly points to fleet coordination,

53
00:03:24,564 --> 00:03:29,914
scalability, robustness, and human robot
collaboration as an ongoing challenges.

54
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:34,746
That combination is exactly where
static om- automation breaks down.

55
00:03:35,765 --> 00:03:39,700
The more capable the system
becomes, the more important it is

56
00:03:39,705 --> 00:03:41,704
to control the authority at runtime.

57
00:03:42,491 --> 00:03:47,174
In a logistic network, one wrong
automated action can create a

58
00:03:47,174 --> 00:03:51,837
secondary consequences, which will be
more expensive than a original error

59
00:03:52,837 --> 00:03:58,096
Before we build anything, let me show
you what a wall looks like without gate.

60
00:03:59,155 --> 00:04:01,783
This is hypothetical, not a real incident.

61
00:04:02,189 --> 00:04:05,300
I have built it from the failure
modes we will cover in this talk.

62
00:04:07,045 --> 00:04:09,646
A carrier suspends service on a busy lane.

63
00:04:11,401 --> 00:04:14,810
When routing agent proposes a
reroute with ninety-nine percent

64
00:04:14,818 --> 00:04:20,063
confidence, that sounds great, except
it's based on its thin history.

65
00:04:21,474 --> 00:04:24,849
With no gate, ninety-nine percent
is treated as a permission,

66
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:26,574
and the booking tool runs.

67
00:04:27,246 --> 00:04:31,868
Minutes later, an agent nobody
registered calls the same carrier API.

68
00:04:32,399 --> 00:04:35,138
Now, those two plans are
competing for the same capacity.

69
00:04:36,103 --> 00:04:37,615
Someone flips the flag.

70
00:04:38,635 --> 00:04:43,071
New runs stop, but the runs that are
already in flight, they keep going.

71
00:04:43,721 --> 00:04:49,428
And when the review starts, the logs show
what happened, but not why it was allowed.

72
00:04:50,707 --> 00:04:55,077
Every missing control on this slide,
we will discuss later in the talk

73
00:04:56,077 --> 00:04:58,203
Where is the bridge
between those two worlds?

74
00:04:58,930 --> 00:04:59,965
Think of an airport.

75
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:03,856
A feature flag decides whether
this terminal is open at all.

76
00:05:04,832 --> 00:05:08,376
Gate checks every boarding pass
before anyone gets onto the plane.

77
00:05:09,032 --> 00:05:11,357
You need both in order
to get onto the plane.

78
00:05:12,436 --> 00:05:16,668
A flag answers, is this
autonomous capability available

79
00:05:16,705 --> 00:05:19,887
in any environment, region, or
workflow wherever you're running?

80
00:05:21,049 --> 00:05:23,753
Next comes what I call
it as a autonomy policy.

81
00:05:24,832 --> 00:05:27,508
It's the contract between
exposure and authority.

82
00:05:28,621 --> 00:05:33,154
Which tools are allowed, which decision
class it covers, and a minimum confidence

83
00:05:33,166 --> 00:05:39,051
floor, maximum action cost, a rollout, an
expiry date, and a kill switch condition.

84
00:05:40,163 --> 00:05:42,106
Then the agent proposes an action.

85
00:05:42,861 --> 00:05:45,354
The agent evaluates that specific case.

86
00:05:45,883 --> 00:05:49,010
Only after that does the tool
boundary execute anything.

87
00:05:50,008 --> 00:05:51,191
Two planes, one loop.

88
00:05:52,051 --> 00:05:56,363
Those are the two questions from a few
minutes ago drawn as an architecture here.

89
00:05:57,364 --> 00:06:01,253
You keep shipping continuously,
you control exposure progressively,

90
00:06:01,453 --> 00:06:04,222
and per decision governance
stays on the whole time.

91
00:06:05,394 --> 00:06:08,092
The flag isn't a safety system here.

92
00:06:09,032 --> 00:06:11,125
It is one part of the control system

93
00:06:12,125 --> 00:06:13,899
The architecture in one picture.

94
00:06:15,380 --> 00:06:18,717
Intelligent logistic
orchestration architecture.

95
00:06:20,159 --> 00:06:23,332
I'll go bottom to top, and I
want you to watch layer three.

96
00:06:24,686 --> 00:06:26,438
Layer one is infrastructure.

97
00:06:27,267 --> 00:06:32,996
A scalable cloud tier, event streaming,
and low-latency edge tier for decisions

98
00:06:33,037 --> 00:06:34,263
that can't wait on a round trip.

99
00:06:35,702 --> 00:06:38,752
Layer two is agent intelligence.

100
00:06:39,281 --> 00:06:44,111
Multi-agent coordination with centralized
training and decentralized execution.

101
00:06:44,808 --> 00:06:49,400
Plus LLM reasoning loop and an
orchestration tools and memory.

102
00:06:51,292 --> 00:06:57,445
Layer four is where your logistics lives,
warehouse, routing, demand, replenishment,

103
00:06:57,541 --> 00:06:59,440
all everything comes in your layer four.

104
00:07:01,109 --> 00:07:02,627
Now the layer three, governance.

105
00:07:03,876 --> 00:07:09,674
It intercepts every agent-proposed
action, applies the confidence threshold,

106
00:07:10,674 --> 00:07:14,614
checks the safety classification,
and enforces security boundary.

107
00:07:15,527 --> 00:07:17,349
Every action passes through here.

108
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:20,287
See the control panel on the right.

109
00:07:21,273 --> 00:07:23,507
That's what I'm adding for this talk.

110
00:07:23,869 --> 00:07:29,820
Feature flags, confidence, stakes,
novelty, identity, and rollback.

111
00:07:31,332 --> 00:07:34,066
They aren't six separate mechanisms.

112
00:07:34,404 --> 00:07:36,921
They are one runtime authority system.

113
00:07:38,909 --> 00:07:44,631
So the implementation question becomes,
can I prove, observe, and reverse the

114
00:07:44,671 --> 00:07:47,036
authority I have granted to an agent?

115
00:07:48,036 --> 00:07:51,185
Let's follow a single
event through the system.

116
00:07:52,534 --> 00:07:57,177
A shipment update or demand signal or
sensor reading or something happens.

117
00:07:58,437 --> 00:08:02,569
Agent proposes an action and
attach, attaches a confidence score.

118
00:08:03,901 --> 00:08:08,518
The dominance layer reads the score
alongside stakes and novelty and

119
00:08:08,576 --> 00:08:10,255
turns it into a routing decision.

120
00:08:11,950 --> 00:08:12,823
Take the Route 1.

121
00:08:13,310 --> 00:08:14,715
The decision is routine.

122
00:08:15,017 --> 00:08:18,693
It isn't safety-critical and
confidence clears the threshold.

123
00:08:19,672 --> 00:08:21,213
Then it executes autonomously.

124
00:08:22,204 --> 00:08:25,885
Route 2, it's little broader,
so it goes to a person with

125
00:08:25,893 --> 00:08:27,657
structured evidence package.

126
00:08:29,030 --> 00:08:34,457
Route 3, it's safety-critical and
time-sensitive, so it goes to a

127
00:08:34,483 --> 00:08:39,455
pre-validated edge rule set instead
of waiting on a human or a cloud.

128
00:08:40,976 --> 00:08:43,039
One more detail I care about here.

129
00:08:43,621 --> 00:08:47,719
If a human doesn't respond, the
default is safe state inaction.

130
00:08:49,025 --> 00:08:51,943
No autonomous execution on silence.

131
00:08:52,661 --> 00:08:57,701
That's a design choice, not a
claim about what any law requires.

132
00:08:58,943 --> 00:09:03,307
We just don't want a missing human
to quietly turn into a permission.

133
00:09:04,795 --> 00:09:06,421
Authority is explicit.

134
00:09:07,425 --> 00:09:09,094
It cannot be intercepted

135
00:09:10,094 --> 00:09:11,714
Here is the mistake I see a lot.

136
00:09:12,491 --> 00:09:17,981
A model returns 99% confidence,
and somebody treats that

137
00:09:17,981 --> 00:09:19,430
number like a permission slip.

138
00:09:21,070 --> 00:09:22,789
Confidence is a routing signal.

139
00:09:23,061 --> 00:09:24,498
It's not an authority.

140
00:09:26,128 --> 00:09:27,742
Take a simulated scenario here.

141
00:09:28,052 --> 00:09:34,101
The threshold is 78% and, three
candidates come with 89, 69 and 54.

142
00:09:35,396 --> 00:09:38,880
Only 81 can run autonomously,
the other two can't.

143
00:09:39,744 --> 00:09:42,300
But even that isn't the whole policy.

144
00:09:43,152 --> 00:09:47,394
Confidence tells us how much
evidence the system believes it has.

145
00:09:48,557 --> 00:09:51,697
Takes tell us how much
errors we can tolerate.

146
00:09:52,745 --> 00:09:56,566
Novelty tells us how far the
situation sits from anything

147
00:09:56,568 --> 00:09:58,017
the system has seen before.

148
00:09:59,420 --> 00:10:04,495
So 99% on irreversible safety critical
action still shouldn't sail through.

149
00:10:05,810 --> 00:10:10,470
A thin history should push towards
escalation even if the score looks good.

150
00:10:12,349 --> 00:10:17,042
Treat LLM confidence as an input to
the policy, not a magic permission.

151
00:10:17,998 --> 00:10:22,746
A score only useful if it is
calibrated properly, so we have

152
00:10:22,748 --> 00:10:24,370
to monitor it for the drift

153
00:10:25,370 --> 00:10:30,162
Nobody wants a job that is just
clicking approve on whatever AI says.

154
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,373
So let's give people a
clear contract instead.

155
00:10:35,514 --> 00:10:36,193
Advisory.

156
00:10:37,322 --> 00:10:42,513
Human decides and AI analyzes for
high stakes and high novelty calls.

157
00:10:43,177 --> 00:10:44,388
Human in the loop shape.

158
00:10:45,285 --> 00:10:50,797
AI brings the analysis and the evidence,
and human adjudicates the exception.

159
00:10:51,803 --> 00:10:52,649
Delegative.

160
00:10:53,935 --> 00:10:58,832
AI executes routine high confidence,
low novelty decisions while humans

161
00:10:58,834 --> 00:11:01,546
monitor it, and supervised autonomous.

162
00:11:02,199 --> 00:11:07,727
For safety critical, time critical
actions, a pre-validated edge rule set

163
00:11:08,035 --> 00:11:14,001
acts inside a policy, and people supervise
the policy rather than every event.

164
00:11:15,233 --> 00:11:16,900
Notice what we avoided here.

165
00:11:17,278 --> 00:11:19,455
One single human in the loop switch.

166
00:11:20,832 --> 00:11:25,357
Here, it depends on stakes,
confidence, novelty, and decision.

167
00:11:27,306 --> 00:11:29,021
Flag fits naturally here.

168
00:11:30,176 --> 00:11:33,970
A flag can control whether a
decision class is currently

169
00:11:34,027 --> 00:11:35,688
admitted to a delegative mode.

170
00:11:37,315 --> 00:11:42,229
While the per decision gate still
decides whether an individual case runs.

171
00:11:43,055 --> 00:11:45,547
The goal here isn't maximum autonomy.

172
00:11:46,049 --> 00:11:51,463
It is the right authority for each
decision Now let's make this concrete.

173
00:11:52,917 --> 00:11:56,704
A useful autonomy flag
isn't true or false.

174
00:11:57,426 --> 00:11:59,278
It's a versioned policy object.

175
00:12:00,659 --> 00:12:01,848
Look at the card here.

176
00:12:02,719 --> 00:12:08,254
Enable true, mode delegative, rollout ten
percent, confidence floor of seventy-eight

177
00:12:08,267 --> 00:12:11,070
percent, allow list of tools here.

178
00:12:11,942 --> 00:12:13,613
Let's assume route and booking.

179
00:12:13,816 --> 00:12:16,067
Cost ceiling, five hundred dollars.

180
00:12:16,156 --> 00:12:17,164
No alt limit.

181
00:12:17,973 --> 00:12:22,771
Kill switch condition that fires
on an incident, and an expiry date.

182
00:12:23,634 --> 00:12:27,318
Honestly, this schema is proposed
pattern for this talk, not a standard.

183
00:12:27,739 --> 00:12:30,274
What is standard here is an open feature.

184
00:12:31,110 --> 00:12:36,097
A vendor-neutral API for a specific
flagging, so the contract your application

185
00:12:36,156 --> 00:12:42,546
codes against stays portable even if
the flag platform underneath changes.

186
00:12:43,383 --> 00:12:45,124
And here is the deeper point.

187
00:12:46,068 --> 00:12:53,230
Once autonomy is a policy, you can review
it, differentiate it, audit who changed

188
00:12:53,230 --> 00:12:56,096
it, test it in Canary, and roll it back.

189
00:12:57,543 --> 00:13:03,022
That looks more like how we already
run a production configuration than how

190
00:13:03,036 --> 00:13:06,044
we usually talk about model behavior

191
00:13:07,044 --> 00:13:08,587
How do we roll this out?

192
00:13:09,524 --> 00:13:13,200
The way we would roll out any
risky production capability in

193
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:15,271
stages with evidence at each one.

194
00:13:16,916 --> 00:13:20,458
Consider, steps as stages
from observe to delegate.

195
00:13:21,990 --> 00:13:23,608
Page zero, shadow mode.

196
00:13:23,930 --> 00:13:28,512
Agent watches the workflow and makes
prediction, but nothing happens here.

197
00:13:29,451 --> 00:13:30,938
Stage one, advisory.

198
00:13:31,728 --> 00:13:37,451
The AI proposes, human decides,
and every adjudication becomes

199
00:13:37,471 --> 00:13:38,588
training evidence here.

200
00:13:39,692 --> 00:13:42,102
Stage two, guarded delegation.

201
00:13:42,594 --> 00:13:47,857
A narrow cohort, one to 10% gets
autonomous execution with strict tools,

202
00:13:47,859 --> 00:13:49,975
spending limits, and rollback controls.

203
00:13:51,064 --> 00:13:56,074
Stage three, delegation at a scale
with exception monitoring and

204
00:13:56,086 --> 00:13:58,721
governance policy still in place.

205
00:13:59,682 --> 00:14:03,785
What I care more about
is promotion criteria.

206
00:14:04,189 --> 00:14:08,805
They should be object here, not only
model quality, containment evidence.

207
00:14:09,183 --> 00:14:12,517
If we disable the flag, do
active runs actually stop?

208
00:14:12,978 --> 00:14:16,498
If confidence drifts, does
the policy degrade safely?

209
00:14:17,205 --> 00:14:22,624
If the human queue overflows, does
escalation stays inside capacity?

210
00:14:23,271 --> 00:14:27,177
That's what makes this progressive
delivery for autonomy and not

211
00:14:27,181 --> 00:14:29,033
a feature flag around an agent

212
00:14:30,033 --> 00:14:31,107
Let me ask you a question.

213
00:14:32,547 --> 00:14:35,466
When did you last test your
kill switch under load?

214
00:14:37,362 --> 00:14:39,534
Because the flag value is in 10.

215
00:14:40,186 --> 00:14:42,016
Containment is something you prove.

216
00:14:43,651 --> 00:14:47,118
Most flags give you admission control.

217
00:14:47,486 --> 00:14:48,931
New runs gets blocked.

218
00:14:49,690 --> 00:14:54,916
But an autonomous system can have
a work in flight, agent loop plan

219
00:14:55,062 --> 00:15:01,171
or a scheduled job, a queue message
or a cache policy and an open tool

220
00:15:01,177 --> 00:15:04,711
session, async worker with retries.

221
00:15:05,173 --> 00:15:06,375
Flip the flag off now.

222
00:15:07,199 --> 00:15:09,055
Some of that may still carry on.

223
00:15:09,930 --> 00:15:12,491
It's the autonomous version
of a partial shutdown.

224
00:15:13,709 --> 00:15:19,547
So, a real kill switch needs a containment
semantics, cancel or drain the active

225
00:15:19,567 --> 00:15:25,036
runs, revoke tool leases, invalidate
all the authorization which are cached,

226
00:15:25,788 --> 00:15:33,828
block fallback paths, then verify with
data that consequential tools calls

227
00:15:33,866 --> 00:15:39,321
have actually dropped to zero or no, no
calls to the, tools that are not needed.

228
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:46,286
Make that tested in SLO from the
disable event to no consequential tool

229
00:15:46,322 --> 00:15:48,389
calls in under some number of seconds.

230
00:15:49,251 --> 00:15:51,489
The right number depends
on your operation.

231
00:15:52,275 --> 00:15:55,640
It is the same lesson as
everywhere in distributed systems

232
00:15:56,640 --> 00:16:02,636
Imagine you built everything we
just covered, gates, policies,

233
00:16:02,835 --> 00:16:08,098
attested kill switch, and then
an agent nobody registered

234
00:16:08,556 --> 00:16:10,603
calls your carrier API directly.

235
00:16:12,098 --> 00:16:13,210
That's a shadow AI.

236
00:16:14,116 --> 00:16:18,846
An unauthorized or unmonitored
agent making competing decisions

237
00:16:19,068 --> 00:16:22,457
or reaching operational APIs
outside your control path.

238
00:16:23,838 --> 00:16:29,956
If it can reach the same tools, you don't
have one control plane, you have two.

239
00:16:30,489 --> 00:16:35,706
Your only authorized one On
the slide, the authorized agent

240
00:16:35,715 --> 00:16:37,683
goes through a security sidecar.

241
00:16:38,585 --> 00:16:44,449
Signed identity policy checks, MCP schema
validation, prompt injection screening

242
00:16:44,952 --> 00:16:47,283
backed by cryptographic agent registry.

243
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:53,263
The shadow agent fails the identity
check and the response is quarantined.

244
00:16:54,052 --> 00:16:58,409
Block the tool call, log the
event, and notify the owner

245
00:16:59,409 --> 00:17:04,351
If you remember one sentence
from this section, the model is

246
00:17:04,386 --> 00:17:08,275
not trust boundary, the tool is.

247
00:17:10,058 --> 00:17:13,462
The tool is where a proposal
becomes a real-world cost.

248
00:17:14,858 --> 00:17:16,744
So walk the chain on the slide here.

249
00:17:18,322 --> 00:17:21,344
Agent reasons, plans, and proposes.

250
00:17:21,913 --> 00:17:22,927
Then four checks.

251
00:17:23,532 --> 00:17:24,726
One, flag state.

252
00:17:25,587 --> 00:17:27,732
Is this capability enabled?

253
00:17:28,583 --> 00:17:29,513
Two, authority.

254
00:17:30,471 --> 00:17:33,629
Is this case eligible for delegation?

255
00:17:34,029 --> 00:17:34,993
Three, identity.

256
00:17:36,179 --> 00:17:39,279
Which agent is actually making the call?

257
00:17:40,139 --> 00:17:40,844
Four, scope.

258
00:17:42,130 --> 00:17:44,761
Which tool and which
arguments are permitted?

259
00:17:46,089 --> 00:17:50,699
MCP server enforces the last one
with schema, identity, and scope.

260
00:17:51,864 --> 00:17:55,804
That gives you a clean separation
between reasoning and enforcement.

261
00:17:57,110 --> 00:17:59,201
An agent can ask for an action.

262
00:18:00,074 --> 00:18:03,214
It can't grant itself
the authority to perform.

263
00:18:04,516 --> 00:18:09,402
It matters even more as agent identity
matures as a security discipline

264
00:18:10,402 --> 00:18:12,917
Let's test few assumptions.

265
00:18:14,098 --> 00:18:18,699
Here are five that sound reasonable and
don't survive contact with production.

266
00:18:20,210 --> 00:18:24,625
First one, high confidence means
it's safe to act, but in reality,

267
00:18:24,737 --> 00:18:29,061
confidence is a routing signal, but
stakes and novelty decide the rest.

268
00:18:30,016 --> 00:18:33,159
Myth two, put human in
every loop, you are safe.

269
00:18:33,504 --> 00:18:35,523
In reality, escalation is scarce.

270
00:18:35,967 --> 00:18:39,563
We cannot push everything to the
human in the loop, so authority

271
00:18:39,563 --> 00:18:41,498
mode has to match the decision.

272
00:18:42,498 --> 00:18:44,370
A feature flag is a kill switch.

273
00:18:45,151 --> 00:18:50,897
In reality, if flag value is intent,
containment is something you prove.

274
00:18:52,275 --> 00:18:53,221
Another one comes here.

275
00:18:53,319 --> 00:18:56,225
Our network perimeter covers every agent.

276
00:18:57,198 --> 00:19:01,625
In reality, an unregistered agent can
reach some of the tools, so identity

277
00:19:01,629 --> 00:19:03,735
has to be enforced at a tool level.

278
00:19:05,055 --> 00:19:09,552
Myth five, guardrails
belong in the prompt.

279
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:14,504
In reality, model isn't the
trust boundary, the tool is.

280
00:19:14,836 --> 00:19:17,411
The tools which are exposed
to be your trust boundary.

281
00:19:19,034 --> 00:19:21,745
If one of those made you wince, good.

282
00:19:22,604 --> 00:19:26,234
That's exactly what our
next set of slides gonna be

283
00:19:27,234 --> 00:19:29,152
Picture, one of the incident review.

284
00:19:29,349 --> 00:19:34,715
Something went wrong and someone asked
the question that matters: why was the

285
00:19:34,716 --> 00:19:37,053
agent allowed to do at this moment?

286
00:19:38,073 --> 00:19:41,918
For autonomous system, logging the
final action is not at all enough.

287
00:19:41,978 --> 00:19:43,494
We need the authority trail.

288
00:19:44,152 --> 00:19:49,091
A useful event record capture the
agent identity, feature flag version,

289
00:19:49,817 --> 00:19:55,402
confidence takes, novelty, decision,
what are the collaborations made,

290
00:19:55,533 --> 00:19:57,758
what is the tool scope and outcome.

291
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:03,654
If any of the action escalated to
human adjudication or what is the

292
00:20:03,686 --> 00:20:05,511
review time from human in the loop.

293
00:20:06,298 --> 00:20:11,247
This lets an engineer answer the
incident question: why was this agent

294
00:20:11,384 --> 00:20:13,323
allowed to do that at that moment?

295
00:20:14,515 --> 00:20:18,332
It also lets governance teams
measure whether the system is

296
00:20:18,345 --> 00:20:20,417
behaving within the policy envelope.

297
00:20:21,859 --> 00:20:26,779
Flag state plus decision gate
plus tool identity plus outcome.

298
00:20:27,973 --> 00:20:32,352
All these equals to your
reconstructable autonomy

299
00:20:33,352 --> 00:20:36,318
Now, the person on the
other end of escalation.

300
00:20:37,529 --> 00:20:39,950
Human-in-the-loop is not a free resource.

301
00:20:40,697 --> 00:20:45,505
If a system escalates everything
uncertain, the review queue

302
00:20:45,506 --> 00:20:47,172
becomes the new bottleneck.

303
00:20:48,197 --> 00:20:52,499
Human performance varies with workload,
shift duration, and competing tasks.

304
00:20:54,269 --> 00:20:58,905
A dominance policy that overloads
operator can fail even when

305
00:20:58,905 --> 00:21:00,915
the AI is correct on average.

306
00:21:01,989 --> 00:21:07,124
If the queue is saturated, the system is
not meaningfully human-in-the-loop anymore

307
00:21:08,124 --> 00:21:13,653
It's time for a self-check So
where is your autonomy today?

308
00:21:15,591 --> 00:21:21,495
Number one, can you list every agent
that can call your operational tools?

309
00:21:23,265 --> 00:21:26,802
Two, does each one have
its own signed identity?

310
00:21:28,350 --> 00:21:34,168
Three, is autonomy a versioned policy
with an owner and an expiry date?

311
00:21:35,273 --> 00:21:39,851
Four, have you tested your
kill switch under load?

312
00:21:40,851 --> 00:21:46,232
Five, do your logs capture the flag
version, confidence, and authority mode?

313
00:21:48,116 --> 00:21:53,163
Six, do you track escalation,
queue depth, and confidence drift?

314
00:21:55,109 --> 00:21:58,642
Out of six question,
count your asses here.

315
00:22:00,294 --> 00:22:04,197
If it is zero to two,
start with the foundation.

316
00:22:05,659 --> 00:22:09,989
If it is three to four, you are
ready to run in advisory mode.

317
00:22:11,639 --> 00:22:16,095
If it is five to six, you can
start planning guarded delegation.

318
00:22:17,101 --> 00:22:20,437
This is just an illustrative
self-check, not a standard,

319
00:22:20,962 --> 00:22:23,078
and your score isn't grade.

320
00:22:23,197 --> 00:22:28,119
It simply tells you where to begin, and
that's exactly what next slide is about

321
00:22:29,119 --> 00:22:32,147
Practical 90-day path to
the governed autonomy.

322
00:22:33,288 --> 00:22:38,016
This is the implementation version
I would put in front of the platform

323
00:22:38,020 --> 00:22:39,781
or logistics engineering teams.

324
00:22:41,083 --> 00:22:43,956
Day zero to 30, it will be a foundation.

325
00:22:44,414 --> 00:22:49,093
Unify your events, build an agent
inventory, signed identities,

326
00:22:49,811 --> 00:22:53,434
tool inventory, one standard
autonomic policy contract.

327
00:22:54,313 --> 00:22:57,136
Note what's missing from
the list, the model.

328
00:22:58,428 --> 00:23:01,777
Don't start with model, start
with the control surface.

329
00:23:03,682 --> 00:23:06,651
Day 31 to 60, advisory.

330
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:12,344
Let agents generate recommendation,
build the calibration report dataset,

331
00:23:13,886 --> 00:23:19,065
instrument the review experience,
and define SLOs for accuracy,

332
00:23:19,219 --> 00:23:21,301
escalation, latency, and containment.

333
00:23:22,851 --> 00:23:28,032
Day 61 to 90, begin guarded
delegation at tiny cohort.

334
00:23:28,557 --> 00:23:31,254
Use feature flags to control exposure.

335
00:23:32,256 --> 00:23:35,873
But, keep the decision
gate active on every event.

336
00:23:36,305 --> 00:23:37,756
Run the kill switch drill.

337
00:23:38,492 --> 00:23:39,799
Exercise rollback.

338
00:23:40,182 --> 00:23:41,773
Watch your confidence drift.

339
00:23:42,587 --> 00:23:44,423
The promotion rule here is simple.

340
00:23:44,898 --> 00:23:48,219
Autonomy is not promoted
because demo loop.

341
00:23:49,378 --> 00:23:54,331
It is promoted because the system
demonstrated that it can be

342
00:23:54,331 --> 00:23:56,593
contained, observed, and governed

343
00:23:57,593 --> 00:24:02,615
If you can't measure the line
between allowed to act and actually

344
00:24:02,659 --> 00:24:05,479
acted, you are not ready to scale.

345
00:24:06,617 --> 00:24:08,582
Here are five SLOs.

346
00:24:10,284 --> 00:24:11,610
Decision quality.

347
00:24:12,533 --> 00:24:13,991
Was the action correct?

348
00:24:14,848 --> 00:24:20,041
Look at the outcome and defect
rates Second one, escalation help.

349
00:24:21,292 --> 00:24:23,515
Can humans observe the queue?

350
00:24:24,736 --> 00:24:26,623
Queue depth and review SLA.

351
00:24:27,828 --> 00:24:28,828
Re-containment.

352
00:24:30,452 --> 00:24:32,476
Does half really mean half?

353
00:24:33,542 --> 00:24:35,261
Time to zero tool calls.

354
00:24:37,147 --> 00:24:39,181
Four, policy drift.

355
00:24:39,849 --> 00:24:41,959
Has the operating regime changed?

356
00:24:42,463 --> 00:24:44,550
Watch confidence and novelty drift.

357
00:24:46,091 --> 00:24:47,989
Five, authority integrity.

358
00:24:48,441 --> 00:24:51,770
Did the action match the
policy active at the time?

359
00:24:52,492 --> 00:24:54,519
Flags, scope, and identity

360
00:24:55,519 --> 00:24:56,208
Quick test here.

361
00:24:57,311 --> 00:25:01,044
Only one of those five
is a classic model metric

362
00:25:02,044 --> 00:25:03,425
It's a decision quality.

363
00:25:04,848 --> 00:25:09,920
The other four are systems and governance
metrics That's the difference between

364
00:25:09,922 --> 00:25:14,762
deploying an agent and operating
autonomy as a production capability.

365
00:25:15,889 --> 00:25:22,208
A model can be accurate while the system
is still unsafe to scale because the

366
00:25:22,215 --> 00:25:27,885
rollba- rollback path is incomplete
or the escalation queue is overloaded

367
00:25:28,885 --> 00:25:31,828
Let's zoom out and put
it all in one slide now.

368
00:25:32,295 --> 00:25:37,202
If you screenshot only one slide
from this talk, make this one, a

369
00:25:37,202 --> 00:25:39,730
production-ready autonomy control plane.

370
00:25:40,807 --> 00:25:43,127
The top row in this path is decision.

371
00:25:44,887 --> 00:25:49,461
Feature flag control plane decides
what capability is exposed.

372
00:25:50,601 --> 00:25:52,447
Agent's runtime proposes work.

373
00:25:53,214 --> 00:25:56,034
Decision gate evaluates authority.

374
00:25:56,649 --> 00:26:02,242
MCP and identity enforce the tool
boundary And the operation tools

375
00:26:02,243 --> 00:26:03,781
create the real-world effect.

376
00:26:05,037 --> 00:26:07,929
The bottom row is loop
that keeps it honest.

377
00:26:08,784 --> 00:26:13,267
Observability captures
events, traces, flag changes.

378
00:26:13,674 --> 00:26:14,917
Then you evaluate.

379
00:26:15,555 --> 00:26:19,787
Evaluation tells us whether
behavior is staying inside the SLOs.

380
00:26:20,893 --> 00:26:25,591
And finally, the human op handle
exceptions and update policy.

381
00:26:26,853 --> 00:26:29,982
And rollback closes the loop,
disable, drain, and verify.

382
00:26:31,156 --> 00:26:35,454
The key idea here is these
aren't separate dashboards.

383
00:26:35,741 --> 00:26:37,469
They share identifiers.

384
00:26:38,135 --> 00:26:43,877
The same policy version, agent
identity, decision identity, and trace

385
00:26:43,882 --> 00:26:47,775
should connect the flag event, the
governance decision, and tool call.

386
00:26:48,738 --> 00:26:52,084
That gives you one answer to
the hardest production question:

387
00:26:53,171 --> 00:26:55,299
What authority did agent have?

388
00:26:56,273 --> 00:26:57,486
Why did it have it?

389
00:26:58,030 --> 00:26:59,300
And what happened next?

390
00:27:00,181 --> 00:27:01,397
The loop is your product

391
00:27:02,397 --> 00:27:04,561
Let me go back to where we started.

392
00:27:05,702 --> 00:27:09,330
The hard question was never can
an agent make this decision.

393
00:27:10,382 --> 00:27:15,052
It was how we turn the autonomy
on without turning off control.

394
00:27:16,150 --> 00:27:19,023
Three, ideas to take with
you from this session.

395
00:27:19,500 --> 00:27:21,138
One, get the capability.

396
00:27:22,062 --> 00:27:25,525
Feature flags give you
progressive exposure and rollback.

397
00:27:26,296 --> 00:27:27,781
Second, get the decision.

398
00:27:28,641 --> 00:27:34,194
Confidence matters, but combine it with
stakes, novelty, and explicit policies.

399
00:27:35,122 --> 00:27:41,878
Three, through the boundary, identify
tool authorizations, reconstructible

400
00:27:41,926 --> 00:27:46,341
evidence, and a contain- containment
path you have actually tested.

401
00:27:47,848 --> 00:27:51,271
The goal here isn't a human
approving every transaction.

402
00:27:51,284 --> 00:27:53,503
That would throw away
the value of autonomy.

403
00:27:53,979 --> 00:28:00,005
The goal is explicit authority and a way
to stop when virtual reality changes.

404
00:28:01,018 --> 00:28:02,699
Autonomy here is not a Boolean.

405
00:28:03,115 --> 00:28:08,018
It's a production feature with
a policy, rollout strategy, SLO,

406
00:28:08,528 --> 00:28:10,398
your audit trail, kill switch.

407
00:28:11,821 --> 00:28:15,711
The future of logistics is
not a human or autonomous.

408
00:28:16,409 --> 00:28:18,090
It's a governed collaboration.

409
00:28:19,369 --> 00:28:23,033
Thank you, and enjoy your
rest of your conference.

